{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 cb console\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One ReaImGui window that sends the open project, or the selected MIDI item, to a language model you run yourself and shows the answer. REAPER stays usable while a request runs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b cb_console.lua} opens the window, or closes it when it is open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b cb_narrator.lua} opens it on the Narrator tab: a short description of the project.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b cb_midi_variation.lua} opens it on the MIDI variation tab: one variation of the selected MIDI item, added to the item as a new take.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Needs Windows, PowerShell 7 at its default path, ReaImGui 0.10 or newer (ReaTeam Extensions repository) and an OpenAI-compatible chat endpoint such as Ollama. Anthropic's Claude models can be added beside it, with your own API key.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Setup: copy {\f1 bridge/bridge.config.example.json} to {\f1 bridge/bridge.config.json} in the same folder and set {\f1 baseUrl} and {\f1 model}. ReaPack never updates or removes your copy. Details in the repository's README.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 cb-reapack\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 ReaPack repository for {\b cb console}, a REAPER window that sends the open project, or the selected MIDI item, to a language model you run yourself, and shows the answer (REAPER stays usable while a request runs), and for two JSFX: {\b cb pump} and {\b cb 808}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 cb console\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The package {\f1 cb_console.lua} (category LLM Bridge) adds three actions:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b cb_console.lua} opens the window, or closes it when it is open.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b cb_narrator.lua} opens it on the Narrator tab. Run describes the project: tempo, tracks, their FX and items, markers and regions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b cb_midi_variation.lua} opens it on the MIDI variation tab. Run adds one variation of the selected MIDI item to that item as a new take; the original take is kept.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Windows (tested on Windows 11). The bridge runs in a hidden console window through {\f1 conhost.exe --headless}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab PowerShell 7 at its default path, {\f1 C:\\Program Files\\PowerShell\\7\\pwsh.exe} ({\f1 winget install Microsoft.PowerShell}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab ReaImGui 0.10 or newer, from the ReaTeam Extensions repository that ReaPack lists by default.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab An OpenAI-compatible chat completions endpoint, for example {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ollama.com"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Ollama
}}}
 on the same machine.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Install\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab In REAPER: Extensions > ReaPack > Import repositories, and paste {\f1 https://github.com/coltonbearden/cb-reapack/raw/main/index.xml}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Extensions > ReaPack > Browse packages, install {\f1 cb console: LLM narrator and MIDI variation}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab Install ReaImGui the same way if it is not installed yet.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The files land in {\f1 <REAPER resource path>\\Scripts\\cb-reapack\\LLM Bridge\\} (Options > Show REAPER resource path in explorer/finder).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Set up the model\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The package ships {\f1 bridge\\bridge.config.example.json}, which points at Ollama on this machine:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 \{\line
  "baseUrl": "http://localhost:11434/v1",\line
  "model": "gpt-oss:20b",\line
  "models": ["gpt-oss:20b"],\line
  ...\line
\}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With Ollama installed, {\f1 ollama pull gpt-oss:20b} is all it needs. To use another endpoint or model, copy {\f1 bridge.config.example.json} to {\f1 bridge.config.json} in the same folder and edit the copy: {\f1 baseUrl} (the bridge appends {\f1 /chat/completions}), {\f1 model}, and {\f1 models} (the models the window offers). The bridge reads {\f1 bridge.config.json} when it exists and the example otherwise. ReaPack never updates or removes your copy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the endpoint needs an API key, put it alone in the file {\f1 %LOCALAPPDATA%\\reaper-bridge\\token}; it is sent as a Bearer token to that endpoint only, and never logged.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Claude models (optional)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 cb console can also ask Anthropic's Claude models, billed to your own API key from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://platform.claude.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Claude Console
}}}
. Nothing about them is switched on by the package: the example config lists Ollama only.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab Store your key. In a PowerShell 7 window:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi0 \f1 pwsh -NoProfile -File "<REAPER resource path>\\Scripts\\cb-reapack\\LLM Bridge\\bridge\\Set-BridgeKey.ps1"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi0 It asks for the key at a hidden prompt and keeps it encrypted to your Windows user (DPAPI) in {\f1 %LOCALAPPDATA%\\reaper-bridge\\anthropic.key}, then checks it against the API's model list, which costs nothing. {\f1 -Status} says whether a key is stored, {\f1 -Probe} checks it again, {\f1 -Remove} deletes it, and {\f1 -FromStdin} reads it from a password manager's command line instead of the prompt. The key is never printed, logged or written into a job folder.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab List the models in your own {\f1 bridge.config.json}. A model entry may be an object that names its provider and carries its own settings:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi0 \f1 \{\line
  "providers": \{\line
    "ollama": \{ "baseUrl": "http://localhost:11434/v1" \},\line
    "anthropic": \{\}\line
  \},\line
  "model": "gpt-oss:20b",\line
  "models": [\line
    "gpt-oss:20b",\line
    \{ "id": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "provider": "anthropic", "reasoningEffort": "high",\line
      "maxTokens": 16000, "price": \{ "inputPerMTok": 2, "outputPerMTok": 10 \} \}\line
  ],\line
  ...the rest as in the example\line
\}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi0 Set {\f1 model} to a Claude model's id to make it the default.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\f1 reasoningEffort} (low, medium, high) becomes Claude's effort setting; the window's Reasoning effort picker marks each model's own default.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\f1 maxTokens} counts Claude's thinking too, so give it room (16000 is plenty here).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\f1 httpTimeoutSeconds} is how long the bridge waits for that model; the window gives up 10 s later. Models without one wait 50 s, as before. In the author's measurements (2026-10-22, 567 runs) Claude Opus, Sonnet and Haiku 5.5 always answered within 31 s, so they need none; a large local model may.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\f1 price} (USD per million input and output tokens, from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Anthropic's pricing page
}}}
) is used only for the cost estimate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\f1 actions} overrides any of these for one action, as the top-level {\f1 actions} does.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\f1 temperature} is not sent to Claude models.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab A top-level {\f1 baseUrl}, as in the example, still works for Ollama.\sa180\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The window lists a Claude model only while a key is stored. If a Claude model is picked anyway (as the default model, say) with no key stored, the status line says so and nothing is sent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 What is sent and kept\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A request carries a summary of the project (narrator) or the notes of the selected MIDI item (variation), plus the instruction you type. With a Claude model the same request goes to Anthropic's API under your key. Each request is a folder under {\f1 %LOCALAPPDATA%\\reaper-bridge\\jobs\\} holding {\f1 request.json}, {\f1 response.json} and {\f1 bridge.log}; the response and the log record the provider, the tokens and an estimated cost, which the window's log shows too. These folders are kept so a result can be looked at again; delete them whenever you like.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 JSFX\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two plain JS effects. They need nothing else and run on every platform REAPER runs on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b cb pump} ({\f1 cb_pump.jsfx}, category Dynamics): a tempo-synced volume shaper, the house and EDM "pump" without sidechain routing. The gain reaches its minimum exactly on the beat and comes back over the release; rate from 1/16T to 1 bar, four shapes, or one dip per MIDI note.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b cb 808} ({\f1 cb_808.jsfx}, category Instruments): a mono 808 and sub-bass instrument with a pitch drop at the attack, glide, drive and a lowpass tone control. Its tail ends in exact silence.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Install them from Extensions > ReaPack > Browse packages as above. They land in {\f1 <REAPER resource path>\\Effects\\cb-reapack\\Dynamics\\} and {\f1 Effects\\cb-reapack\\Instruments\\}, and show in the FX browser as {\f1 JS: cb pump} and {\f1 JS: cb 808}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Report problems at https://github.com/coltonbearden/cb-reapack/issues\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Licence\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 MIT, see {\f1 LICENSE}.\par}
}
